How do we evaluate a limit like

$$
\displaystyle \lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{x^2}{\sqrt{9x^4+x}}
$$

Because the ${\color{blue}{x^4}}$ in the radical is a perfect square, we can factor it outside of the radical.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{x \to  \infty}\frac{x^{2}}{\sqrt{9x^{4} + x}} &= \lim_{x \to  \infty}(x^{2})/(\sqrt{x^{4}(9 + \frac{x}{x^{4}})}) \\
&= \lim_{x \to  \infty}\frac{x^{2}}{\sqrt{x^{4}} \cdot \sqrt{9 + \frac{1}{x^{3}}}} \\
&= \lim_{x \to  \infty}\frac{x^{2}}{\mid x^{2} \mid \sqrt{9 + \frac{1}{x^{3}}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $x^2>0$ for all $x$, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mid x^2 &= x^2
\end{aligned}
$$

Then, we can cancel the $x^2$ in the numerator and denominator, and evaluate the limit.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{x \to  \infty}\frac{x^{2}}{\mid x^{2} \mid \sqrt{9 + \frac{1}{x^{3}}}} &= \lim_{x \to  \infty}\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}\sqrt{9 + \frac{1}{x^{3}}}} \\
&= \lim_{x \to  \infty}\frac{1}{\sqrt{9 + \frac{1}{x^{3}}}} \\
&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{9 + 0}} \\
&= \frac{1}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

